import mundane theorem    stdlib/numbers/exponent-step

theorem minus-one-even-power-natural
  note        Each step of 2 in the exponent multiplies by (−1)·(−1), which is
              1, so (−1)^(2k) stays at its value 1 for k = 0.
  let n ∈ ℕ₀                                                          (H1)
  then (−1)^(2n) = 1

1.  For all k ∈ ℕ₀, (−1)^(2k) = 1.
    induction on k starting at 0

    base
    1.1.  (−1)^(2·0) = 1
          arithmetic

    step
    let k ∈ ℕ₀                                                        (K)
    assume step 1 is true for k, the induction hypothesis             (IH)

    1.2.  2(k + 1) = (2k + 1) + 1
          algebra

    1.3.  (−1)^(2(k + 1)) = (−1)^((2k + 1) + 1)
          substitute 2(k + 1) = (2k + 1) + 1 (line 1.2)

    1.4.  (−1)^((2k + 1) + 1) = (−1)^(2k + 1)·(−1)
          mun:exponent-step a := −1, m := 2k + 1

    1.5.  (−1)^(2k + 1) = (−1)^(2k)·(−1)
          mun:exponent-step a := −1, m := 2k

    1.6.  (−1)^(2k)·(−1) = 1·(−1)
          substitute (−1)^(2k) = 1 (IH)

    1.7.  (−1)^(2k + 1) = −1
          calculation
            (−1)^(2k + 1) = (−1)^(2k)·(−1)     1.5
                          = 1·(−1)             1.6
                          = −1                 arithmetic

    1.8.  (−1)^(2k + 1)·(−1) = (−1)·(−1)
          substitute (−1)^(2k + 1) = −1 (line 1.7)

    1.9.  (−1)^(2(k + 1)) = 1
          calculation
            (−1)^(2(k + 1)) = (−1)^((2k + 1) + 1)     1.3
                            = (−1)^(2k + 1)·(−1)      1.4
                            = (−1)·(−1)               1.8
                            = 1                       arithmetic

2.  (−1)^(2n) = 1
    instantiate k := n in line 1
